 1 big thing: Meta&#x27;s AI app takes off 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images Meta's Muse is rocketing up the app charts with a distinctly practical vision for AI — an agent that can shop, haggle, cancel subscriptions and clear away the digital drudgery of everyday life, Axios' Zachary Basu writes. 

 Mark Zuckerberg is staking Meta's AI comeback on one of the enduring strengths of his internet empire: making new technology feel native to billions of ordinary people. 🤳 Ten days after launch, Muse is the No. 1 free iPhone app in America — ahead of ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude and Instagram.

 Muse pairs a highly polished interface — built around a cuddly, customizable avatar — with serious computing power, including its own secure virtual computer and browser in the cloud. Users give it tasks in plain English, while Muse itself surfaces ideas, goals and suggestions that teach people about its capabilities as it works across websites and connected accounts. 🤖 Muse poses an early test of whether Meta's structural advantages — product design, distribution and an intimate grasp of consumer behavior — can shape the AI race more than raw intelligence alone.

 🤔 Reality check: The more useful Muse becomes, the more of your life it needs to see: email, calendars, financial accounts, purchases, contacts and eventually even the phone calls it can make in your name.

 Zuckerberg has repeatedly tried to reinvent both Meta and himself, from Facebook founder to metaverse evangelist to AI optimist. A deep reservoir of public distrust has proven far harder to shed. Go deeper . 

 
 
